  "Block-not-end-to-end framing attacks an undocumented contract. Zenoh docs say 'the node will wait' (singular, local); CongestionControl and Reliability are separate orthogonal knobs. The packet frames Block as a promised end-to-end anti-drop guarantee that 'measurably fails' — but 4/5 reviewers independently concluded that promise was never made, making this partly a strawman (novelty_killer, evaluation_prosecutor, theory_skeptic, product_realist all flag this).",
  "Per-hop≠end-to-end is the DEFAULT prediction for any hop-by-hop overlay without an explicit end-to-end credit/ack protocol (Saltzer/Reed/Clark 1984). The 3-node router result confirms standard queueing behavior — it does not falsify a documented contract or reveal a new mechanism. This is the gate-4 threat and it is NOT rebutted by evidence in the packet. The packet's counter ('genuinely novel per-hop fabric, not inherited DDS/TCP') is assertion, not argument — hop-by-hop overlays with per-link backpressure predate zenoh by decades.",
  "The mature-lineage inversion argument ('DDS/MQTT are end-to-end; zenoh inverted to per-hop') is overstated. MQTT QoS 0/1 drops at broker; DDS Best-Effort drops. The lineage is mixed, not uniformly end-to-end, weakening the 'novel inversion' claim (theory_skeptic)."
]

