PRIOR_ART_ADEQUATE: no
  — novelty_killer explicitly reports both external search tools were permission-denied; no live prior-art search was completed. Domain-knowledge-based assessment was substituted but does not meet the ≥2 independent retrievable sources standard for certifying collision OR clearance. Saltzer-1984 collision is conceptually identified but not verified against the specific zenoh composition in retrievable sources. Per committee rules, GREEN cannot be emitted without adequate prior-art search.

RATIONALE:
Unanimous YELLOW across all 5 reviewers. No reviewer found a fatal collision with ≥2 retrievable sources (RED barred), but no reviewer found the contribution clears GREEN either. The committee converges on a consistent diagnosis:

(1) THE GENUINE CONTRIBUTION IS REAL BUT THIN. The composition — silent put-Ok + zero debug logs (logger-proven-functional) + split-default contract-flip between get(Block) and put(Drop) — is an undocumented UX/API hazard in a tool that is now the default ROS 2 transport. This is operationally meaningful, especially for safety-critical robotics where silent data loss is unacceptable. No reviewer disputes this leg.

(2) THE MECHANISM FRAMING OVERCLAIMS. The marquee finding — "Block measurably fails end-to-end, proven with 3-node router topology" — is rebutted by 4/5 reviewers on two independent grounds: (a) Block was never documented as end-to-end (zenoh separates CongestionControl from Reliability as orthogonal knobs), so "fails its contract" attacks a contract that was not made; (b) per-hop backpressure not propagating through an intermediate router is the default prediction from Saltzer/Reed/Clark 1984 for any hop-by-hop overlay without explicit end-to-end credit, so the 3-node result confirms expected-by-architecture behavior rather than discovering a mechanism surprise. The packet's counter — "zenoh's genuinely novel per-hop fabric, not inherited DDS/TCP" — is assertion without argument; hop-by-hop overlays with per-link flow control predate zenoh by decades.

(3) GATE-4 VERDICT: closer to "best-effort drops + hop-by-hop fabric, both predicted; the composition is a real docs/UX hazard but not a mechanism surprise." The 3-node proof is rigorous empirical work but it confirms a theoretical prediction, it does not overturn one. The contribution is a characterization-level finding — YELLOW, not GREEN.

(4) PATH TO GREEN requires: (a) reframing against Saltzer-1984 with explicit non-obvious delta; (b) correcting Block-vs-Reliability framing against zenoh's own docs; (c) running the 4-node killer experiment to disambiguate "failure" from "deliberate isolation feature"; (d) mandatory baselines (DDS Reliable, MQTT QoS 0/1, NATS core) on the same topology; (e) statistical repeats with CIs; (f) completed live prior-art search. Without these, the claim remains a valid YELLOW-level characterization finding that merits a zenoh issue and docs PR, but does not clear the bar for a GREEN-level novel discovery.

Prompt version: v001. Committee #1 complete; all 5 reviewer votes aggregated; no RED unrebutted; YELLOW sustained.
